or-party candidates.", "watch": "The nuclear innovation campus controversy expanding into a broader anti-incumbent story, or anti-Little voters consolidating behind independent John Stegner, could narrow the margin, though a genuinely competitive race would require an extraordinary shift.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Brad Little (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Idaho's governor race pairs two-term Republican incumbent Brad Little with Democratic small-business owner Terri Pickens, plus independent John Stegner, Libertarian Paul Sand, and Constitution candidate Pro-Life. Little won renomination in May and holds the fundraising advantage Republicans enjoy in every statewide contest this year. The state's Republican lean of roughly eighteen points frames the contest.\n\nPolling is sparse but consistent: a September survey showed Little ahead by ten points, and a July poll by twenty-one. All seven expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets price Little's chances near ninety-eight percent. Challengers have raised the nuclear innovation campus deal as an issue, and a dispute between Pickens and Stegner over campaign tactics has drawn local attention, but nothing suggests a competitive race. The realistic question is the size of Little's winning margin, not who wins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0111, "margin": -14.27}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0115, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0087, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0158, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0168, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0122, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.015, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0193, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0165, "margin": -14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0058, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0101, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-10